# Room Name - TryHackMe: DetectMare
Date: 23 August 2026
Difficulty: Medium
Category: Detection Engineering / Sigma / Splunk / Detection-as-Code

# Scenario
TSS was engaged by the Meridian Defense Research Institute following a full APT21 kill chain that ran undetected from initial access through credential harvesting, lateral movement, and data staging. The attacker gained initial access via a spearphishing attachment, established a foothold using a NetTraveler-style loader, mapped the domain, dumped credentials from memory, moved laterally onto classified file servers using stolen hashes, and staged classified weapons-program design files into encrypted archives for exfiltration. Not a single alert fired across the entire estate. The task was to review five CSIRT-submitted Sigma rule pull requests in the Detection-as-Code pipeline, identify why each rule failed, and tune them to achieve 100% true positive detection with zero false positives.

