# Key Learning
Detection rules written without validating against real environment logs will almost always generate false positives in production, because every environment has legitimate processes that partially match attacker behaviour. Environment-specific context (backup agents, deployment tools, CAD auto-save routines) must be understood before writing exclusion filters, and those filters must be as specific as possible to avoid creating bypass opportunities. Keying on command-line syntax patterns rather than binary names is more resilient against renamed-tool evasion. Each PR in this room required a different failure mode to be diagnosed: wrong field names, missing legitimate process filters, insufficient GrantedAccess scoping, service account exclusion gaps, and binary name dependence — reinforcing that detection engineering is an iterative, evidence-driven discipline rather than a one-shot rule-writing exercise.

# What I Would Flag in a Real SOC
- A Sigma rule that fires zero alerts during a confirmed full kill chain is a detection coverage failure that should trigger a mandatory post-incident rule review across all stages of the MITRE ATT&CK framework
- LSASS access with GrantedAccess 0x1fffff from any process other than known-good security tooling should be treated as a critical credential theft indicator requiring immediate endpoint isolation
- NTLM logons with KeyLength 0 from user accounts (as opposed to service accounts) to sensitive servers are a high-confidence pass-the-hash indicator, Kerberos should be enforced wherever possible to eliminate this attack vector
- Archiving tools executing from AppData\Local\Temp with password flags against network shares containing classified file extensions should trigger immediate data loss prevention review regardless of whether the binary name is recognised
- A service created within minutes of a pass-the-hash logon on a file server is a persistence indicator that should be correlated automatically in the SIEM and escalated without waiting for analyst review
- Detection-as-Code pipelines with red team bypass testing built into the CI/CD process are essential for catching rules that pass static validation but fail against real adversary tradecraft variations
